1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,262
The disagreement was about ownership, and it had a famous ancestor.

2
00:00:04,262 --> 00:00:11,686
“After a year of work, who has the right to the patent?” the chief of the local committee asked.

3
00:00:11,686 --> 00:00:20,365
“The navy has a rule for a wreck, a dictator has a rule for a ballot, and a diplomat has a rule for a tyre.

4
00:00:20,365 --> 00:00:29,599
We have none.” A woman in the front row began to explain the distinction, and the room went quiet.

5
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:41,692
Generally speaking, the team wanted a salary, a wage, and a share; specifically, they wanted the right to privatize nothing and to inherit the name.

6
00:00:41,692 --> 00:00:59,625
“A privatization is not a pardon,” Aisha said, “and a patent is not a reward.” A warrior of the old school, a well-known local businessman, and a selfless volunteer all nodded, which was disappointing to nobody.

7
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:09,508
They could not obtain the forthcoming decision in writing, and the sum was allegedly unpaid, but that was to be expected.

8
00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:15,608
In accordance with their own rule, they tossed a coin instead of taking a vote.

9
00:01:15,608 --> 00:01:25,351
The result was a historic one for the area: the right would be held by the people who used the road, not by the company that had built it.

10
00:01:25,351 --> 00:01:29,720
“We are not solely a service,” Maya said.

11
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:36,225
“We are also a habit, and a habit is harder to punish than a crime.”

12
00:01:36,225 --> 00:01:43,139
After a week of talking, the chief dictated a short clause and the local people signed it.

13
00:01:43,139 --> 00:01:51,588
“Yourselves, not the wireless, not the webcast, and not the gadgetry,” she said.

14
00:01:51,588 --> 00:02:05,361
“You cannot leave an old-fashioned promise to a machine.” A considerate neighbour brought woolen blankets, and a fluent speaker of three languages read the clause aloud to the queue.

15
00:02:05,361 --> 00:02:19,659
They agreed to spend the money sparingly and to keep one exclamation out of the report: “At last!” It was an altogether better ending than a quarrel, and their friend the motel owner said so.

16
00:02:19,659 --> 00:02:28,993
A child from the night queue, a local car owner, and a woman who had lived there all her life stood behind them at the counter.

17
00:02:28,993 --> 00:02:35,548
The people at home always have the last word, and that was fine.
